<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
        App Sandbox is DISABLED.

        Hourglass reads ~/Library/Messages/chat.db, which lives outside any
        sandbox container. macOS gates access via TCC (Full Disk Access), which
        the user grants in System Settings → Privacy & Security → Full Disk Access.

        If we ever enable the sandbox, we'd need a security-scoped bookmark to
        chat.db, which the user would have to re-pick on every macOS update.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
    <false/>

    <!--
        Network client entitlement: enabled ONLY so the first-run NL-search
        model download can reach Hugging Face. The model (~1 GB
        Qwen 2.5-1.5B-Instruct-4bit MLX format) is fetched once and cached
        under the standard Hugging Face cache path; after download, the NL
        agent runs entirely offline against local Metal kernels (verified by
        disconnecting the network and confirming inference still works).

        Today this is mostly future-proofing: with App Sandbox off above,
        macOS doesn't enforce this entitlement on Debug builds. But:
          - We want this scoped so when we eventually ship a signed +
            sandboxed Release build (Distribution → Mac App Store, or even
            DMG with a tighter hardened-runtime profile), the download path
            still works without any further entitlement plumbing.
          - It documents intent to future code reviewers: "yes we make
            exactly one outbound HTTP, and it's for the model download".

        No `com.apple.security.network.server` — we never listen.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
